A||N||12||Dave85||Changing tranny filter||||5||
Z||000000||Dave85||01-03-2000||04:24 PM||||<BR>The Haynes manual says that I need a special bolt. It says to replace bolt A with the special bolt. Is this necessary? I assume the filter just pulls off, right? Can I find the filter at the local parts store? <BR>Thanks<BR>Dave||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||01-03-2000||05:09 PM||||The crazy bolt thing is to assist in draining the pan. Seems like a hassle, since I just pump out the pan through the dipstick hole prior to removal.<P>And, yes, the filter is easy to remove. Basically swing it out of the way of the "retainer" (or whatever it's called...small piece of metal that supports the end of the filter opposite the inlet) and pull down to remove it. When reinstalling, the new filter will have a "grommet" on the end...just push the filter on.<P>Yes, the filters are available just about anywhere. A good filter and gasket should be about $10.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dave85||01-03-2000||07:44 PM||||Tank yu berry mush!||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||01-03-2000||08:44 PM||||I recomend you search the old forum posts. I've covered this a couple times. I'll add a few more notes here.<P>You have to watch out for a couple minor anoyances. You can also pump down the tranny to drain a good portion of the torque converter. <P>you should also blow out the cooler lines and cooler. You can do this from the back of the car. don't fool with the front. you can end up with a radiator leak really easy fooling with the front.<P>If you do everything you can remove 5-6 quarts of fluid. If you only pull the pan you will replace about 3.<P>The altered bolt is handy for removing a stuck pan. It your pan wants to drop easily leave 3 loose bolts, one at each corner, untill all the others are out. This rule and the altered bolt option are to prevent bending the pan.<P>You also want a LARGE drain pan. at least 18inches across the opening. You'll never hit a small one with all the oil.<P>There should be a magnet in the pan. clean it and return it to the lump in the pan. the hole centers on the lump.||||reg||1
Z||000004||johnmac||01-04-2000||04:40 AM||||Now you guys tell me. I am right now in the midst of changing my auto trans oil. I removed all the pan bolts to facilitate removal - - and guess what - -  the whole bloody thing fell down.<P>I now have a garage floor cover with oil. Whooo - is I stupid!!!<P>ONLY REMOVE SEVERAL BOLTS NOT ALL.<P>Question - what's the best stuff to absorb oil and were I can get it?||||reg||1
Z||000005||DJRice||01-04-2000||02:23 PM||||It seems like most auto parts stores have thier own brand of OilDry type stuff that should work.  But you can also use cat litter if there is not any oil dry around.<P>As for getting the stains up...good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000006||deceler8||01-04-2000||03:24 PM||||Hate to differ with Ogre, but I recovered at least 4 and possibly 5 quarts of ATF when I dropped my pan to change the filter.<P>I left the pan off and let it drip down for about 30 minutes before I replaced the filter and pan.<P>If only GM had had the foresight to put a drain plug on the 125's...||||reg||10
Z||000007||theogre||01-04-2000||08:45 PM||||decel, I put in a little over 6 quarts when I last did mine. total volume is a little over 7 I think.<P>Dave85, Cat litter will pull up allot of it. let it stand then stir it up with the brrom and let it stand again. do that a few times.<P>To clean try liquid laundry soap. get it wet, then soap it, you don't need to use the whole bottle. work it in with a deck brush or push broom. let is soak awhile. keep it wet. Then hose the whole mess off.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||01-04-2000||10:34 PM||||Cat litter does wonders for cleaning up oil spills. I remember one time I was letting a friend change his oil at my place in the drive. Well, he failed to notice the old oil filter gasket was still on the block as he was putting on the new one. He filled it all up, started the engine and out spewed near 4-1/2 quarts by the time we figured out what that "sounds like water dripping" sound was. What a mess, plus the driveway very slightly runs down to the street. We had quite a time cleaning it up, took a whole bag of cat litter.||||reg||10
Z||000009||Monkeyman||01-05-2000||05:43 AM||||All kitty litter is not the same!!!  Make sure you use the clay type.  The old generic stuff works the best (the kind with all of the dust!).  I know this from experience!||||reg||1
Z||000010||DRH||01-05-2000||08:29 PM||||Monkeyman is right... when using kitty litter for oil dry "cheaper is better".<P>Also, after you get most of it up... you can get quite a bit more by putting down some more litter (or oil dry) and scrubbing it around with your feet.<P>Bleach will get rid of some of the stain on concrete. There are some acid based concrete cleaning products that I've heard will get most of the stain out. For me, I don't worry about the stain since I'm just going to spill something else later anyhow.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||sdm1964||01-05-2000||11:17 PM||||Here is a tip to get the stains up off the floor,<BR>dry up as much oil as possible, get a brick and put some fresh kitty litter, oil dry etc and use the brick to grind it into the floof, then sone simple green or engine cleaner, presto..... clean floor with little oil marks left.||||reg||14
Z||000012||olympic||01-06-2000||04:14 PM||||  Plain old saw dust from a lumberyard works great on oil spills.  Just take a shovel and a few garbage bags and they'll usually let you take as much as you want.<BR>   I've used this method to "flush" an automatic transmission. Remove the pan, drain oil and change filter. Reinstall pan and fill with new fluid. Unhook the return trans cooler line and clamp a few feet of clear hose on the end, put the other end in a drain pan. Start the car and pour new ATF down the fill tube as fast as you can.(a second person to help is better). When the oil in the clear hose looks new shut the car off, reattach the cooler line, start the car again shifting through all the gears and then check the fluid level.Top off the ATF if necessary. <BR>     BTW, a mechanic told me that they use a special chemical to flush your trans that is suppose to "demagnetize" your tranny, allowing any loose metal particles to be flushed out.  Does anyone think this is true or just another snake oil story??  Also, has anyone seen a good filter you could install in the cooler line? Preferably one with a changeable filter element.||||reg||1
